        <div id="what-small-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Small Should Mean: Pints, Not Footprint
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pints Removed, Not Box Size</h3>

          <p>
            Dehumidifiers are rated in pints of water removed per day, and that number, not the size of the box, is what decides whether a unit will dry your space. The trouble with the small end of the market is that it spans two completely different machines. Compressor dehumidifiers, the kind with a refrigerant coil, start at about 20 pints a day under the Department of Energy's 2019 test procedure. Thermoelectric units, also called Peltier or mini dehumidifiers, are rated in ounces, typically somewhere between 8 and 25 ounces a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ounces Versus Pints</h3>

          <p>
            There are 16 fluid ounces in a pint. So a mini unit rated at 16 ounces a day is a one-pint dehumidifier, and a 20-pint compressor unit removes roughly twenty times as much. Neither is wrong; they are tools for different jobs. A one-pint unit can keep a closet, a gun cabinet or a small cupboard from smelling musty. A 20-pint unit can keep a damp bedroom, a home office or a small finished basement room at 50% relative humidity through a humid summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Renewable Desiccant Boxes</h3>

          <p>
            A third category, renewable desiccant boxes like the Eva-Dry E-333, has no fan and no power cord while it works. It soaks up a few ounces of moisture over weeks, then you plug it in to dry the beads out. It is the right tool for an enclosed space, such as a closet or a safe, and nothing else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bedrooms and Bathrooms Have Their Own Guides</h3>

          <p>
            If you are drying a whole bedroom or a bathroom specifically, we have dedicated guides for those: our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/">bedroom dehumidifier guide</a> ranks units by rated noise, and the <a href="/best-bathroom-dehumidifier/">bathroom dehumidifier guide</a> starts with the exhaust fan. This page is about choosing the right type and size for small spaces generally, from a closet to an RV to a spare room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Closet or cabinet: mini. A room you live in: compressor.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the space has a door you keep shut and is smaller than a walk-in closet, a Peltier or desiccant unit is enough. If it is a room with people, showers or cooking in it, start at a 20-pint compressor unit.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

