          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Small Space Produces</h3>

          <p>
            Now consider what a small space produces. A single shower can put a significant amount of water into a bathroom's air in ten minutes, and a person sleeping in a closed bedroom adds moisture all night through breathing. A typical damp closet against a cold exterior wall, by contrast, gains moisture slowly, through the wall and from damp clothes. That is why a one-pint mini can keep a closet fresh while doing very little in a bathroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rating Conditions Widen the Gap</h3>

          <p>
            The rating conditions widen the gap further. A thermoelectric unit rated at 86 degrees and 80% humidity will remove noticeably less water in a 68-degree room at 60% humidity, because thermoelectric cooling plates condense less water when the air is cooler and drier. A compressor unit's DOE rating is already measured at 65 degrees. So in a typical air-conditioned or heated room, the real gap between a mini unit and a 20-pint compressor is larger than the ratings alone suggest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Mini Units Still Make Sense</h3>

          <p>
            None of this makes mini units bad. They are quiet, light, cheap to run and small enough to put on a shelf. They are just not room dehumidifiers in the sense most people mean, and buying one for a damp bedroom almost always ends in disappointment. If a space smells musty, grows mildew on walls or fogs windows, start at 20 pints.
          </p>
