        <div id="three-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Peltier, Compressor or Desiccant: How Each Works
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thermoelectric (Peltier)</h3>

          <p>
            A thermoelectric dehumidifier uses a Peltier module, a solid-state chip that gets cold on one side when current runs through it. A small fan pulls air across the cold side, moisture condenses, and it drips into a tank. There is no compressor and no refrigerant, so the units are light, nearly silent and simple. The trade-off is that a Peltier chip moves very little heat, which limits how much water it can condense.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compressor</h3>

          <p>
            A compressor dehumidifier works like a small air conditioner with both coils in one box. Refrigerant chills an evaporator coil well below the air's dew point, water condenses on it, and the heat is dumped back into the room through a condenser coil. It removes far more water per watt than a Peltier unit, but it is heavier, louder and costs more up front. It also warms the room slightly, which matters in a small space in summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Desiccant</h3>

          <p>
            A desiccant dehumidifier uses a material that absorbs moisture directly, usually silica gel. The simple renewable kind, like the E-333, has no fan at all; it absorbs moisture until its indicator beads change colour, then you plug it into a wall outlet to heat the beads and drive the water off. Larger electric desiccant units use a rotating wheel and a heater and work well in cold spaces, but they are a different product category and use more power than most small-space owners want.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Three Types Compared</h3>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
