            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For most people, the decision is not really about technology. It is about the size of the space and whether anyone lives in it. Enclosed storage gets a desiccant box or a mini Peltier; a lived-in room gets a compressor unit; and the gap between them, a small bathroom or an RV bedroom, is where the larger Peltier units like the EDV-2400 can make sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sizing a Small Dehumidifier by Space
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Closets and cabinets</h3>

          <p>
            A reach-in closet is usually 50 to 150 cubic feet; a walk-in closet a few hundred. For a shut closet with a mild musty smell, a renewable desiccant box like the E-333, rated for up to 333 cubic feet, is often enough, and it needs no outlet while it works. If the closet is against an exterior wall or below grade and stays damp, step up to a plugged-in mini like the EDV-1100, which runs continuously and empties into a tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bathrooms</h3>

          <p>
            A small bathroom with a working exhaust fan rarely needs a dehumidifier. One with a weak or missing fan needs real capacity, because a shower releases a lot of water quickly, and a mini unit cannot keep up. A compact compressor is the realistic choice for an unventilated bathroom; a larger Peltier like the EDV-2400 can help in a half bath or a small en suite. Our bathroom guide covers the fan-first approach in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bedrooms and home offices</h3>

          <p>
            A 10-by-12 foot bedroom with an 8-foot ceiling holds about 960 cubic feet of air, which is inside the coverage figure of several mini units on paper. In practice, a sleeping adult, a closed door and a humid summer are more than a Peltier unit can handle. A 20 to 22 pint compressor unit is the right starting size for any bedroom that feels clammy. If noise is your main worry, read our bedroom guide, which ranks units by their published decibel figures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">RVs, campers and boats</h3>

          <p>
            RVs generate a surprising amount of moisture in a small volume: cooking, showering and several people breathing in a space of a few hundred square feet. When parked on shore power in humid weather, a compact compressor unit dries an RV much faster than a mini. For storage periods, or for a wardrobe or under-bed locker, a Peltier mini or a renewable desiccant box is the practical choice, since they draw little power and need no attention for days or weeks. In cold storage, see the temperature section below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="energy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Energy Use and Running Cost
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Low Wattage on Paper</h3>

          <p>
            Mini dehumidifiers are often sold as energy efficient because their wattage is low. The EDV-1100 draws about 22 watts according to retailer spec sheets, and the EDV-2400 about 60 watts. The Midea Cube 20 is listed at 214 watts. On wattage alone, the mini units look far cheaper.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Efficiency per Pint Removed</h3>

          <p>
            Per pint of water removed, it is the other way round. The Midea MAD22C1AWS's ENERGY STAR listing gives an Integrated Energy Factor of 1.7 litres per kilowatt-hour, measured under the DOE test. The EDV-2400, at about 25 ounces a day on 60 watts, works out to roughly 0.74 litres from 1.44 kilowatt-hours if it runs all day, or about 0.5 litres per kilowatt-hour, and that is at its own warmer, more favourable rating conditions. A compressor unit removes several times more water for each unit of electricity.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Spec Sheet Arithmetic</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1.7 L/kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Midea 22-pint ENERGY STAR IEF</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~0.5 L/kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EDV-2400, from 25 oz/day at 60W</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1.44 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EDV-2400 running 24 hours</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5.1 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Midea Cube 20 at 214W for 24 hours</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Run Time Decides the Bill</h3>

          <p>
            What matters for your bill is how long the unit runs. A mini Peltier with no humidistat runs nonstop, so 60 watts for 24 hours is about 1.44 kilowatt-hours a day. A compressor unit with a humidistat cycles off once the room reaches the set point, so its daily use depends on how damp the space is. In a small, reasonably sealed room it may run a fraction of the day. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> walks through the calculation with your local rate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Closet Versus Room</h3>

          <p>
            The honest summary: for a closet, a mini unit's low draw is a real benefit, because the job is small and the unit never needs to run hard. For a room, a compressor unit costs more per hour but less per pint, and it actually reaches the humidity target, which a mini unit in an undersized role may never do while running all day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cold Rooms, Winter Storage and RVs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compressor Units in Cool Air</h3>

          <p>
            All dehumidifiers lose capacity as the air gets cooler, but they lose it in different ways. Compressor units struggle below roughly 65 degrees, when the evaporator coil can frost over. Both Midea compressor units here have defrost and auto-restart functions, which help in cooler rooms, but a compressor unit in a 50-degree space will still remove much less water than its rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thermoelectric Units in Cool Air</h3>

          <p>
            Thermoelectric units also lose output in cool air, because the cold side of the Peltier chip is only a little colder than the room, and cool air carries less moisture to condense. Their ratings at 86 degrees and 80% humidity are close to the best case. In an unheated cottage or a winterised RV, a mini Peltier unit may collect very little water at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Desiccant Keeps Working in the Cold</h3>

          <p>
            Desiccant is the type that keeps working in the cold, because it absorbs moisture chemically rather than condensing it. For a closet in an unheated cabin, a boat cabin in winter or an RV in storage, a renewable desiccant box is often the most reliable small option. It will not dry a whole RV, but it will protect a wardrobe, a cupboard or a bag of linens.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Crawl Spaces and Unheated Basements</h3>

          <p>
            If you need to dry a cold space properly, such as a crawl space or an unheated basement, a small unit is the wrong tool. Look at the low-temperature units in our <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">crawl space dehumidifier guide</a> instead, which are designed to work well below the temperatures where small compressor and Peltier units give up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tanks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Tanks, Draining and Placement
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small Tanks Mean Frequent Emptying</h3>

          <p>
            Tank size is where small dehumidifiers become a chore. The EDV-1100 holds about 16 ounces, the PB-03 about 1.5 litres and the EDV-2400 about 1.8 litres. At their rated output those tanks last one to two days, and all three switch off automatically when full. That is fine in a closet you check weekly only if the unit collects less than its rating, which in a cool closet it often does. In a damp space, expect to empty a mini unit every day or two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compressor Buckets and Continuous Drainage</h3>

          <p>
            Compressor units have larger buckets and can usually drain continuously through a hose to a floor drain or sink. The Midea Cube is notable here: Midea says its extended tank is three times bigger than those in conventional dehumidifiers, and the unit's tank is built into the body rather than sliding out the front. If you cannot run a hose, check the bucket size before you buy and plan to empty it daily in summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Placement</h3>

          <p>
            Placement matters more in a small space than a large one. Give the unit a few inches of clearance on the intake and exhaust sides, keep it away from curtains and clothing, and close the door to the space you are drying. A dehumidifier in a closet with the door open is trying to dry the whole house. For a compressor unit in a bedroom or office, put it where the warm exhaust will not blow on you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Set a Humidity Target</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, set a target. Aim for 45 to 50% relative humidity in a small living space and below 50% in a closet or storage area. A compressor unit with a humidistat holds that automatically. A mini unit without one simply runs, so a cheap standalone hygrometer is worth buying to check whether it is keeping up. If the reading never drops, the unit is too small for the space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When a Small Dehumidifier Is the Wrong Choice
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Basements</h3>

          <p>
            Small dehumidifiers fail in three predictable situations. The first is a basement. Even a small finished basement room shares air with the rest of the below-grade space and pulls moisture through concrete walls and floors. A 20-pint compressor unit may cope in a sealed room; most basements need 35 to 50 pints. See our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> for sizing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole Floors and Open-Plan Spaces</h3>

          <p>
            The second is a whole floor or open-plan space. A small unit placed in an open room is trying to dry everything connected to it, and it will run constantly without reaching the target. If you want to dry the main living area of a home, start with our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">home dehumidifier guide</a>, which sizes units from 22 to 50 pints by the space they serve.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Source Problem</h3>

          <p>
            The third is a source problem. A closet that is damp because of a plumbing leak, a bathroom with no exhaust fan, or a bedroom wall with condensation from a failed vapour barrier will not be fixed by any dehumidifier, small or large. Fix the source first; a dehumidifier is for the moisture that remains.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Small Is the Right Size</h3>

          <p>
            If none of those apply and your space really is small, the right unit is usually obvious once you think in pints. A closet needs a fraction of a pint a day; a bedroom or office needs ten to twenty. Buy to the job, not to the box size, and a small dehumidifier will do exactly what it says. For a full walk-through of the other features worth paying for, read <a href="/how-to-choose-a-dehumidifier/">how to choose a dehumidifier</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do mini dehumidifiers actually work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for the right job. A thermoelectric mini rated at 8 to 25 ounces a day can keep a closet, cabinet or small storage room from going musty. It will not dry a bedroom or bathroom in active use, because those spaces generate more moisture than a mini removes. For a room people live in, a 20-pint compressor unit is the realistic minimum.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for a small room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a closed closet or cabinet, a mini Peltier or desiccant unit is enough. For a bedroom, office or small bathroom without good ventilation, start at 20 to 22 pints a day on the 2019 DOE rating. A 22-pint compressor unit like the Midea MAD22C1AWS is rated for far larger spaces, so it has headroom in a small room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a small dehumidifier cheap to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A mini Peltier unit draws about 22 to 60 watts, so even running nonstop it uses roughly 0.5 to 1.5 kilowatt-hours a day. A compressor unit draws around 200 watts but cycles off at its set point and removes several times more water per kilowatt-hour. For a room, the compressor unit is usually cheaper per pint removed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a small dehumidifier in an RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. On shore power in humid weather, a compact 20 to 22 pint compressor unit dries an RV fastest. For storage or for wardrobes and lockers, a Peltier mini or a renewable desiccant box draws little power and needs little attention. In cold storage, choose desiccant, because Peltier and compressor units lose most of their output in cold air.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are thermoelectric dehumidifiers quiet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes. With no compressor, the only moving part is a small fan, and retailers list units like the Eva-Dry EDV-1100 at about 33 decibels. That makes them easy to live with in a closet or bedroom. Compressor units are louder, and our bedroom dehumidifier guide ranks them by their published noise figures if quiet is the priority.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do I empty a mini dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At their rated output, 16-ounce to 1.8-litre tanks last about one to two days in a damp space, and they switch off automatically when full. In a cool, mildly damp closet they collect less and may go several days. If you are emptying it more than once a day, the space likely needs a compressor unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best dehumidifier for a closet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a shut closet with no outlet, a renewable desiccant box like the Eva-Dry E-333 is simplest. For a damp closet against an exterior wall or below grade with an outlet nearby, a plugged-in mini like the Eva-Dry EDV-1100 runs continuously. Keep the door closed and check humidity with a hygrometer; aim to keep it below 50%.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Mini Unit for a Bedroom</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thermoelectric unit rated at about a pint a day cannot keep up with a sleeping adult in a closed room during a humid summer. If the room feels clammy, smells musty or fogs its windows, start with a 20 to 22 pint compressor unit and leave the minis for closets and cabinets.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Ounces With Pints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                There are 16 ounces in a pint, and mini units are rated in ounces under warm, humid conditions while compressor units are rated in pints at 65 degrees. A unit rated at 25 ounces a day removes about 1.6 pints under kinder conditions than a 20-pint unit's rating. Convert before comparing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Door Open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small dehumidifier in a closet or bathroom with the door open is trying to dry the whole house and will run constantly without reaching its target. Close the door to the space you are drying, and if you cannot, size the unit for the bigger connected space instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting Coverage Claims</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small dehumidifier makers quote cubic feet, square feet or both, and the figures often do not match each other or real conditions. Size by pints per day against the moisture load, and check with a cheap hygrometer. If humidity never drops below 50%, the unit is too small.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Peltier Unit in a Cold Space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thermoelectric output falls in cool air, and in an unheated cabin or winterised RV a mini Peltier may collect almost nothing. Use a renewable desiccant box for cold enclosed spaces, or a low-temperature crawl space unit if you need real capacity below 60 degrees.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

